Say WHICH instruction made a bad bus access, and stop capping the count at 64.

WHY. The fork's route into the cartridge's own multiplayer - state 54, the
map/gamemode/time chooser - produces a burst of "[bus] ARM9 unmapped read" and
then the frame loop stops. Four attempts to explain that burst failed, and each
failed the same way: the log gives the ADDRESS and nothing else, so the guest
instruction responsible has to be guessed at. It was guessed at four times.

Worse, the "64" everyone had been counting is this function's own warning cap.
An earlier analysis recorded "exactly 64 reads, always" and built a reading on it - a
bounded 32-node walk - when the truth is that the logger stops after 64. A
diagnostic whose limit gets mistaken for a measurement is worse than none.

So: print the PC, the LR and the low registers, and let the cap be set from the
environment. LSW2_BUS_WARN_LIMIT=0 means no limit; unset keeps the old 64, so a
run that did not ask sees no change other than the extra fields.

g_cpu is already in scope - bus.cpp includes state.h and runtime_arm.h and uses
g_cpu.R[15] a few hundred lines above in the dispatch diagnostic.

--- a/runner/src/bus.cpp
+++ b/runner/src/bus.cpp
@@ -422,13 +422,70 @@
 }
 
 void unmapped(uint32_t addr, bool write, uint32_t width, uint32_t value) {
-    static int warned = 0;
-    if (warned < 64) {
-        std::fprintf(stderr, "[bus] ARM%c unmapped %s 0x%08X w=%u%s\n",
+    // THE LIMIT IS A LIMIT, NOT A MEASUREMENT. It was a hard 64, and a burst
+    // that reached it got read downstream as "exactly 64 accesses" - turning a
+    // truncated log into a false fact about the guest. Say so when it bites,
+    // and let a run that is investigating raise it.
+    static const long limit = [] {
+        if (const char* s = std::getenv("LSW2_BUS_WARN_LIMIT")) {
+            char* end = nullptr;
+            const long n = std::strtol(s, &end, 0);
+            if (end != s) return n;          // 0 means no limit
+        }
+        return 64L;
+    }();
+    static long warned = 0;
+    if (limit == 0 || warned < limit) {
+        // THE PC IS THE WHOLE POINT. An address alone says a pointer was wrong;
+        // the PC says which instruction believed it, and LR says who called
+        // that code. The low registers are what a bad index is built from.
+        std::fprintf(stderr,
+                     "[bus] ARM%c unmapped %s 0x%08X w=%u%s  pc=%08X lr=%08X "
+                     "r0=%08X r1=%08X r2=%08X r3=%08X r4=%08X r5=%08X\n",
                      g_nds_active == NDS_ARM9 ? '9' : '7',
                      write ? "write" : "read", addr, width,
-                     write ? "" : " (â†’0)");
+                     write ? "" : " (->0)",
+                     g_cpu.R[15], g_cpu.R[14], g_cpu.R[0], g_cpu.R[1],
+                     g_cpu.R[2], g_cpu.R[3], g_cpu.R[4], g_cpu.R[5]);
         ++warned;
+        // AND WHAT LED HERE, when a run asked for it: with deep trace on, the
+        // runtime keeps a ring of recent branches, and the first bad access is
+        // the one moment it is worth reading.
+        if (warned == 1 && g_runtime_deep_trace) runtime_trace_dump_recent(96);
+        // AND WHO CALLED IT, without deep trace's cost: the runtime's own stack
+        // of guest return addresses, for the first few reports. The first use
+        // was the lobby return after a four-player match, where the
+        // game's decompressor is handed a source that is not data at all and
+        // pc/lr name only the decompressor. LSW2_BUS_WARN_STACK=1.
+        static const bool stack_on = [] {
+            const char* s = std::getenv("LSW2_BUS_WARN_STACK");
+            return s && s[0] != '\0' && s[0] != '0';
+        }();
+        if (stack_on && warned <= 4) {
+            const uint32_t depth = runtime_call_stack_depth();
+            const uint32_t* ret = runtime_call_stack_data();
+            // PAST THE HELPER'S OWN FRAMES. The ITCM decompressor BLs its bit
+            // refill hundreds of times without unwinding, so the innermost ten
+            // were all its own; after the first few, only main-RAM code is
+            // printed, which is where the caller that handed it the source is.
+            std::fprintf(stderr, "[bus]   call stack (innermost first, depth %u):",
+                         unsigned(depth));
+            uint32_t shown = 0, skipped = 0;
+            for (uint32_t i = 0; i < depth && shown < 16u; ++i) {
+                const uint32_t a = ret[depth - 1u - i];
+                if (i >= 3u && a < 0x02000000u) { ++skipped; continue; }
+                std::fprintf(stderr, " %08X", unsigned(a));
+                ++shown;
+            }
+            std::fprintf(stderr, "  (%u TCM frames skipped)\n", unsigned(skipped));
+        }
+        if (limit != 0 && warned == limit) {
+            std::fprintf(stderr,
+                         "[bus] ... %ld unmapped accesses reported; further "
+                         "ones are NOT shown. That is the log's limit, not the "
+                         "guest's - raise it with LSW2_BUS_WARN_LIMIT (0 = no "
+                         "limit) before counting anything.\n", limit);
+        }
     }
     (void)value;
 }
